    "available_at": "2026-11-27T19:20:07.369025+00:00",
    "body": "King Abdulaziz International Conference Centre, Riyadh. ( Image courtesy of Future Minerals Forum. )\nBy: Staff Writer November 27, 2026\nSaudi Arabia is preparing to postpone its Future Minerals Forum as regional attacks threaten the January gathering, The Financial Times reported Thursday.\nOrganizers have warned speakers and participants that the Riyadh event will likely need to be rescheduled, according to the newspaper.\nThe forum told the newspaper it had not been cancelled and that updates would come through official channels.\nAs of Friday, the event’s website was continuing to advertise the Jan. 12–14 dates.\nThe uncertainty follows a Houthi attack on the headquarters of Saudi Arabia’s Public Investment Fund in Riyadh on Tuesday that injured 11 people, the FT reported.\nOther major Saudi events have already shifted. Cityscape Global’s organizers moved the real estate exhibition from November to Feb. 1–4, 2027. The Global Health Exhibition has also been deferred until October 2027.\nMining ambitions\nSaudi Arabia has hosted five editions of the forum, which brings together governments, mining companies and investors to discuss mineral supply and development.\nThe January 2026 gathering drew 21,500 participants and produced 132 agreements and memorandums of understanding valued at 100 billion Saudi riyals, or about $26.7 billion (C$37.9 billion), organizers said. Its ministerial roundtable included representatives from 91 countries.\nPast speakers included executives from BHP (ASX, LSE: BHP), Rio Tinto (ASX, LSE: RIO), Vale (NYSE: VALE) and Chilean state-owned miner Codelco.\nThe published 2027 program carries the theme “global collaboration for resilient mineral supply,” with a ministerial roundtable and discussions on investment, innovation and minerals policy.\nUnder Vision 2030, Saudi Arabia is seeking to reduce its reliance on oil by expanding other industries. Mining is intended to become a third pillar of industrial development alongside oil and petrochemicals.\nThe forum is part of that effort to attract investment and build mining partnerships across Africa, Western Asia and Central Asia. Print\nCOMMODITY: Copper Gold Nickel Oil and Gas\nREGION: Asia Middle East\nRepublish this article",
